    "costumbres": {
      "c-1b01793c9ceb5fe1": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-138",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "“This expression [play—Heb. tsachek, to laugh; and so rendered in Genesis 21:6] often signifies dancing among the ancients. It probably refers here to some mystic dance which imitated the course of the stars. The sun-god was represented by the ancients by the image of a bull. Its…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-138",
          "sourceHash": "b8fa4f7e6230ad785c0644547f6429619a162e28696556808debdb03956bf6d2",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-53a6f8da9bd070c2": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-137",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Most of the large idols worshiped by the ancients were first made of wood and then covered with plates of metal. We find illustrations of this in Isaiah 30:22 and 40:19. See also Nahum 1:14; Habakkuk 2:18. A wooden image (or one of stone; see Hab. 2:19) was first prepared, and th…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-137",
          "sourceHash": "efd4dbd804115c926892fdb30b1da31b5b21d2b326040e9dce3ba84d7a47c036",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
